{#- The parts every chapter page shares, each written once: the surah header,
    the basmala, the page marker, the hizb sign and the mark that ends an
    ayah. A chapter template imports this file and calls the macros; what
    differs between layouts is passed in, never copied. -#}

{#- The head of a surah: its header and, under it, the basmala line (not
    where the basmala is the surah's first ayah).

    The header: number, name, count of ayahs, as three cells in a row
    between two lines. `latin`: the transliterated name and its meaning
    under the Arabic name (books with a translation). `plain`: the flowing
    books; there a script without the calligraphic name font gets the same
    header made of a div and three spans (see below).

    The name: where the calligraphic name font goes with the Quran text, it
    is that font's drawing: the typed word "surahNNN" becomes the name, and
    the character U+E000 is the word "surah" (dir="ltr" keeps the two in
    that order). Elsewhere it is written out. Its cell (or, in the header
    of div and spans, the div) carries the id "surah-N" in every form.

    WHY THESE ARE STILL TABLES. A reading app stores the place a reader
    stopped at, a bookmark or a highlight as a path through the page: "the
    first table, its first row, its first cell" for the top of a surah's
    first page; "the fifth div" for an ayah of a word-by-word page. A book
    that replaces an older copy is read with the paths saved for the older
    one. So the elements in front of the text, and inside the header, keep
    the kind and the order they have always had: one table for the header,
    one for the basmala. Their look no longer depends on being tables (the
    stylesheet gives each its own size); role="presentation" tells a screen
    reader that they hold no table of data. Inside the name's cell the name
    and the Latin line are two spans, as always; the stylesheet makes each
    a block of its own.

    WHY THE FLOWING WARSH AND INDOPAK BOOKS HAVE A DIV AND SPANS. In the
    released flowing Warsh book the header was the bare name, a div with a
    span, and the top of a surah's first page was stored as "the first
    div, its span, its text". So the framed header there is a div with
    three spans, with no white space between them, and the first span
    starts with its text: that old path still finds the first text of the
    header, on the surah's first page. The stylesheet draws the div as a
    table and each span as a cell, with the rules of the table header. The
    IndoPak book was never released; it has the same markup, so that there
    is one way to write it. -#}
{%- macro surah_head(surah, basmala_text, latin=false, plain=false) -%}
{%- if plain and not use_glyph_fonts -%}
<div class="surah-frame" id="surah-{{ surah.number }}"><span class="header-info">ترتيبها<br/><span class="header-num plain-num">{{ surah.number | arabic_numerals }}</span></span><span class="surah-name"><span class="surah-name-text">سورة {{ surah.name_arabic }}</span></span><span class="header-info">آياتها<br/><span class="header-num plain-num">{{ surah.ayah_count | arabic_numerals }}</span></span></div>
{%- else -%}
<table class="surah-header" role="presentation"><tr>
        <td class="header-info">ترتيبها<br/><span class="header-num plain-num">{{ surah.number | arabic_numerals }}</span></td>
        <td class="surah-name" id="surah-{{ surah.number }}">{% if use_glyph_fonts %}<span class="surah-glyph" dir="ltr">surah{{ '%03d'|format(surah.number) }}&#xE000;</span>{% else %}<span class="surah-name-text">سورة {{ surah.name_arabic }}</span>{% endif %}{% if latin %}<span class="header-transliteration">{{ surah.name_transliteration }}{% if surah.name_translation %} — {{ surah.name_translation }}{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="header-info">آياتها<br/><span class="header-num plain-num">{{ surah.ayah_count | arabic_numerals }}</span></td>
    </tr></table>
{%- endif -%}
{%- if surah.has_bismillah and not surah.bismillah_is_first_ayah %}
    <table class="bismillah-table" role="presentation"><tr><td class="bismillah">{{ basmala_text }}</td></tr></table>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The invisible marker where a page of the printed mushaf starts. -#}
{%- macro page_marker(ayah) -%}
{%- if ayah.page_marker %}<span epub:type="pagebreak" role="doc-pagebreak" id="page{{ ayah.page_marker }}" title="{{ ayah.page_marker }}"></span>{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The hizb sign in front of an ayah, glued to the ayah's first word. In
    flowing text a line may break before the sign.

    A SIGN ADDED TO THE TEXT LATER (`ayah.hizb_marker_added`; today one:
    Warsh 15:22, which the publisher's newer file has and the older did
    not). A reading app stores a place in a flowing surah as "the n-th
    piece of text of the block, so many characters in". Written the usual
    way, a new sign splits one piece into two, and every place saved after
    it in that surah, in a book made from the older text, would point one
    piece off. So in flowing text such a sign carries its own break point
    INSIDE its span, and the ayah before it leaves out the break point it
    would write after its mark (`sign_follows` of ayah_text): the pieces of
    text are as many as before, each as long as before. -#}
{%- macro hizb(ayah, flow=false) -%}
{%- if ayah.hizb_marker and flow and ayah.hizb_marker_added %}<span class="hizb-marker">&#x200B;۞</span>&#8288;
{%- elif ayah.hizb_marker %}{% if flow %}&#x200B;{% endif %}<span class="hizb-marker">۞</span>&#8288;{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The link around the mark that ends an ayah: our own number (scripts whose
    font draws a digit as the ornate circle) or the font's own mark (IndoPak),
    with a word joiner in front that glues it to the ayah's last word.
    `note_href`: the link opens that note; without it the link points at the
    ayah itself, for the plugin (which tells this link by its class
    "ayah-mark-link"). `on_mark`: the ayah's id sits on the mark (flowing
    text). `bare_note_mark`: the older form of a font's own mark as a note
    link, kept for the flowing tap-for-translation layout.

    WHY A PLAIN MARK SITS IN A SPAN OF ITS OWN. Until the summer of 2026 the
    number was a bare span after the ayah's last word, and a reading app
    stores the end of that last word as "the span after it". A highlight of
    a whole ayah made in a book of that time ends there. So the span stays
    the element after the last word (class "ayah-mark-box"), the link is
    inside it, and the number's own box inside the link. A mark that opens
    a note was a link from the start and keeps that form, for the same
    reason. -#}
{%- macro mark_link(surah, ayah, note_href='', on_mark=false, bare_note_mark=false) -%}
{%- set sa = surah.number ~ '-' ~ ayah.ayah_number -%}
{%- set idattr = (' id="ayah-' ~ sa ~ '"') if on_mark else '' -%}
&#8288;
{%- if note_href and bare_note_mark and not render_ayah_numbers -%}
<a class="ayah-noteref ayah-mark" epub:type="noteref" href="{{ note_href }}"{{ idattr }}>{{ ayah.ayah_marker }}</a>
{%- else -%}
{%- if note_href %}<a class="ayah-noteref" epub:type="noteref" href="{{ note_href }}">{% else %}<span class="ayah-mark-box"><a class="ayah-mark-link" href="#ayah-{{ sa }}">{% endif -%}
{%- if render_ayah_numbers %}<span class="ayah-number"{{ idattr }}>{{ ayah.ayah_number | arabic_numerals }}</span>{% else %}<span class="ayah-mark"{{ idattr }}>{{ ayah.ayah_marker }}</span>{% endif -%}
</a>{% if not note_href %}</span>{% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- An ayah's text and the mark that ends it. Where the text ends in a box
    (the overlined words of prostration), box and mark are wrapped so that
    they stay on one line. `flow`: flowing text, where the ayah's id sits on
    the mark and a line may break after it. `sign_follows`: the next ayah
    opens with a hizb sign that was added to the text later; that sign
    brings the break point itself (see the hizb macro). -#}
{%- macro ayah_text(surah, ayah, note_href='', flow=false, bare_note_mark=false, sign_follows=false) -%}
{%- set head, box = ayah.text | final_box -%}
{%- set marked = render_ayah_numbers or ayah.ayah_marker or bare_note_mark -%}
{{ head }}
{%- if box and marked -%}
<span class="ayah-end">{{ box }}{{ mark_link(surah, ayah, note_href, flow, bare_note_mark) }}</span>
{%- else -%}
{{ box }}{% if marked %}{{ mark_link(surah, ayah, note_href, flow, bare_note_mark) }}{% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if flow and marked and not sign_follows %}&#x200B;{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Word by word: the hizb sign and the ayah's mark each stand in a word box
    of their own, so they sit on the line of the Arabic words. -#}
{%- macro hizb_box(ayah) -%}
{%- if ayah.hizb_marker %}        <span class="wbw-word wbw-marker"><span class="wbw-arabic"><span class="hizb-marker">&#x06DE;</span></span></span>&#8288;{% endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro ayah_mark_box(surah, ayah, note_href='') -%}
&#8288;<span class="wbw-word wbw-marker"><span class="wbw-arabic">
{%- if render_ayah_numbers or ayah.ayah_marker -%}
{%- if note_href %}<a class="ayah-noteref" epub:type="noteref" href="{{ note_href }}">{% else %}<a class="ayah-mark-link" href="#ayah-{{ surah.number }}-{{ ayah.ayah_number }}">{% endif -%}
{%- if render_ayah_numbers %}<span class="ayah-number">&#x2060;{{ ayah.ayah_number | arabic_numerals }}</span>{% else %}<span class="ayah-mark">{{ ayah.ayah_marker }}</span>{% endif -%}
</a>
{%- endif -%}
</span></span>
{%- endmacro -%}
